# 0063-secrets-and-exports-review-r2.patch -- the review's two families the earlier patches left: "a secret written at the default mode" and "the old file deleted before the new one is in place", plus a launch sweep of the .part files a kill leaves behind.
# Applies after 0062 (the tree with 0010..0062 applied). UNTESTED ON DEVICE: compiled (dotnet build, ios-arm64) but never run on an iPhone; File.Move(string, string, bool) is the overload 0040 already uses, FileStreamOptions.UnixCreateMode and File.SetUnixFileMode are .NET 7+.
#   * GameAppMain.cs StoreNasPassword (the fallback file used when the Keychain refuses the password, e.g. a SideStore install): the file is created EMPTY with mode 0600 (FileStreamOptions.UnixCreateMode, and File.SetUnixFileMode for a leftover
#     file that keeps its old mode through FileMode.Create), complete file protection is TRIED on it (its result used to be dropped, 'out _'), and then the password goes in. When the protection is not available the file is KEPT, at 0600, and the
#     app log says so ('NAS password stored with mode 0600, data protection not available on this install: <message>'): such an install may have no data protection either, and refusing there would make the NAS password impossible to store.
#     Only a file that cannot be written is deleted: the function returns false and why, and the Save action of ConfigureNas logs it and tells the user (a managed exception out of a UIAlertAction handler would end the app).
#   * GameAppExport.cs Export (the .ipa) and PlaceRomfs (the slim export's game files): File.Delete(old) + File.Move(part, new) -> File.Move(part, new, true), one rename over the old file, as 0040 does elsewhere; PlaceRomfs also removes its
#     .part when the clone/copy/rename fails (a full disk used to leave a multi-GB <name>.romfs.part in Exports/<Name>/).
#   * GameAppUi.cs SaveTitlesFromContent (0051's .titleids.tsv): the same delete-then-move, the same one-rename fix (a site beyond the two the review named).
#   * GameAppMain.cs SweepLeftoverParts, called in ViewDidLoad right after PrepareLog and before LoadCloud / PreparePlayerDocuments / anything that can start a copy. It deletes only the .part files of the families this app's own code writes
#     (CopyIn, CopyWithProgress and Nas.Download of 0040, Export and PlaceRomfs, SaveTitlesFromContent, the save copies of GameAppSaves), by exact suffix, at fixed places and no deeper: Documents/ (<name>.nsp|.xci|.nsz|.nup|.ipa|.romfs.part,
#     prod.keys.part, .titleids.tsv.part), Documents/Exports/ (*.ipa.part), Documents/Exports/<folder>/ (*.romfs.part), Documents/SaveBackups/<folder>/ (*.zip.part whose name is a save copy's: GameSaves.ParseCopyName). Any other name, a folder and a
#     link (also a linked Exports, SaveBackups or game folder) are left alone. One log line per place that had any: the count and the MB, through Write. Once per process. Not swept: Documents/.nsz/*.nsp.part (the chain's own start-clean removes it,
#     crates/ibuilder-pipeline layout nsz_dir), games/<id>/used_shaders.tsv.part, PreparePlayerDocuments' small copies (all rewritten by the next attempt), and the .part files the Rust chain writes in its own folders.
